WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:01.450
Patient and Public Voice
Tim Kelsey

00:00:01.450 --> 00:00:02.450
National Director for Patients and Information
Preamble

00:00:02.450 --> 00:00:04.810
As we have been doing ever since the board
had its first meeting, we have been trying

00:00:04.810 --> 00:00:11.700
to update on the very high priority that NHS
England gives to active transparencies, as

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:18.860
David said earlier, but equally to the genuine,
authentic commitment to public and patient

00:00:18.860 --> 00:00:20.350
participation that we have.

00:00:20.350 --> 00:00:24.680
So this paper brings together a particular
focus on ways in which we are trying to engage

00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:30.620
the public very, very meaningfully in the
planning and decision-making process of NHS

00:00:30.620 --> 00:00:31.620
England.

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:36.810
Citizens' Assembly
Just to break that down into three parts to

00:00:36.810 --> 00:00:40.720
bring out some of the points in the paper,
we start with the Citizens' Assembly, which

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:44.860
is a commitment we made right at the beginning;
the idea that we absolutely recognise the

00:00:44.860 --> 00:00:49.630
importance of us as an organisation being
properly held to account for the decisions

00:00:49.630 --> 00:00:51.090
we make.

00:00:51.090 --> 00:00:55.400
We have tried to do this in a very different
way, so rather than having a view, going and

00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:59.910
testing it, and then setting up some sort
of organisation, what we have actually done

00:00:59.910 --> 00:01:05.049
is to engage, I think, really meaningfully
with literally hundreds of different advocate

00:01:05.049 --> 00:01:10.020
voices, individuals, organisations, to try
to think differently about what this challenge

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:11.020
could look like.

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:15.409
Recent events
We have emerged from the most recent couple

00:01:15.409 --> 00:01:24.130
of days, where we were joined by 450 colleagues
from across the patient and citizen spectrum

00:01:24.130 --> 00:01:28.270
to help us think through what the answer might
be.

00:01:28.270 --> 00:01:36.369
We spent two days together just reflecting
on how participation can be made real.

00:01:36.369 --> 00:01:37.369
I was amazed.

00:01:37.369 --> 00:01:41.170
I think it was the most fantastic evidence
of the willingness of people out there to

00:01:41.170 --> 00:01:44.429
really work with us on developing a communal
future.

00:01:44.429 --> 00:01:50.880
Just to give you a sense, the event generated
7 million Twitter impressions, which is just

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:51.880
amazing.

00:01:51.880 --> 00:01:58.599
That is a real conversation on social media
about the future for NHS participation.

00:01:58.599 --> 00:02:03.490
The event was live streamed for two days,
and it was a genuinely engaging event.

00:02:03.490 --> 00:02:08.110
In total we had on average around 700 people
watching that at any one time, which I think

00:02:08.110 --> 00:02:10.399
is just amazing, again.

00:02:10.399 --> 00:02:13.140
So, first of all, I want to thank all those
people who participated, but I also want to

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:20.720
say that we are making really good progress
towards a concept of an assembly, a network

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:24.100
that will bring patient voice right into the
heart of NHS England.

00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:28.130
I am going to be picking up with Olivia Butterworth,
who was largely responsible for arranging

00:02:28.130 --> 00:02:33.260
the event, and with Victor and Ciarán, on
taking that forward, with an expectation that

00:02:33.260 --> 00:02:39.439
we will be launching something at the expo
that will make real this commitment to participation.

00:02:39.439 --> 00:02:44.750
Future of Healthcare conference
So that was one thing just to highlight to

00:02:44.750 --> 00:02:45.750
the board.

00:02:45.750 --> 00:02:53.280
The second thing in relation to this campaign
to make the social movement in healthcare

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:59.130
real was the Future of Healthcare conference,
which I know a number of you went to.

00:02:59.130 --> 00:03:01.819
I was unfortunately unable to get there, but
Martin McShane, our colleague, did such a

00:03:01.819 --> 00:03:06.810
fantastic job in advertising the commitment
we have to participation.

00:03:06.810 --> 00:03:13.870
That event, which was organised jointly with
UCL in London, brought together a People's

00:03:13.870 --> 00:03:20.030
Panel of 14 voices from across the community
of the NHS, who I think spoke extraordinarily

00:03:20.030 --> 00:03:24.870
eloquently about the challenges they face
and how we can support them in improvement.

00:03:24.870 --> 00:03:27.659
Feedback
There were just two things that I thought

00:03:27.659 --> 00:03:28.830
would be worth reflecting on.

00:03:28.830 --> 00:03:33.409
One was a really extraordinary article written
by Jackie Ashley (Andrew Marr's wife) reflecting

00:03:33.409 --> 00:03:40.299
on her own experience as a carer, and really
thanking NHS England -- in her words, 'all

00:03:40.299 --> 00:03:43.689
credit must go to NHS England for its bravery
in hosting this event'.

00:03:43.689 --> 00:03:49.829
It was very open discussion about the challenges
real people are facing, and her view was that,

00:03:49.829 --> 00:03:55.870
at last, patients are being given the chance
to be properly heard in the design and commissioning

00:03:55.870 --> 00:03:56.870
of services.

00:03:56.870 --> 00:03:57.870
So that was one voice.

00:03:57.870 --> 00:04:04.440
I was subsequently emailed by a GP from Austria
who had happened to go to the event and, in

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:09.040
his words, 'I was so amazed with the degree
to which the NHS is trying to involve patients

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:10.040
and the public.

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:11.969
Are there lessons that I can take home?'

00:04:11.969 --> 00:04:19.520
This is just a small example of something
I think we are on the cusp of making really

00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:20.970
great.

00:04:20.970 --> 00:04:27.140
Transforming participation guidance
The third thing I wanted to highlight in this

00:04:27.140 --> 00:04:33.640
paper was the publication of Transforming
Participation Guidance, which colleagues in

00:04:33.640 --> 00:04:38.980
my team put together very closely with CCG
colleagues, area teams and so on, and with

00:04:38.980 --> 00:04:43.170
the broader public and patient community,
to think really hard about the kind of standard

00:04:43.170 --> 00:04:47.430
we expect commissioners to reach in making
participation real.

00:04:47.430 --> 00:04:49.831
Standardised care plans
Just some of the very important commitments

00:04:49.831 --> 00:04:56.140
we have now made, which we will be following
through to deliver, are really championing

00:04:56.140 --> 00:04:59.410
the commissioning of proper personalised care
planning.

00:04:59.410 --> 00:05:05.910
As you will know, care planning is a fundamentally
important part of delivering properly personalised

00:05:05.910 --> 00:05:08.230
services, particularly to people with long-term
conditions.

00:05:08.230 --> 00:05:12.170
There is an existing commitment that people
with those conditions have a right to a care

00:05:12.170 --> 00:05:13.170
plan.

00:05:13.170 --> 00:05:16.760
Unfortunately in many cases those care plans
are not what you and I would think of as a

00:05:16.760 --> 00:05:20.610
properly personalised, with attention-to-detail
for the individual.

00:05:20.610 --> 00:05:26.880
So we are going to introduce a standard that
will involve some degree of an interactive

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:27.880
component.

00:05:27.880 --> 00:05:32.030
It will allow people to share conversations,
when they wish to, with their clinicians.

00:05:32.030 --> 00:05:36.620
That is an important commitment for us that
we do now become quite intolerant of GPs,

00:05:36.620 --> 00:05:40.580
in particular, and others who do not take
that responsibility seriously.

00:05:40.580 --> 00:05:45.750
So we are supporting them with ways in which
we can make care planning a reality for everybody

00:05:45.750 --> 00:05:47.720
who has a long-term condition.

00:05:47.720 --> 00:05:50.720
Helping commissioners
Equally important are the conversations we

00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:55.530
have had with local commissioners, particularly
CCG colleagues, who are really anxious to

00:05:55.530 --> 00:05:58.650
engage with this agenda but finding it difficult
to know quite what to do.

00:05:58.650 --> 00:06:03.830
They are seeking support really, and learning
what techniques and methods might be deployed,

00:06:03.830 --> 00:06:09.640
such as doing things like just listen harder,
market research, or indeed modern techniques

00:06:09.640 --> 00:06:13.280
of engagement, like understanding more about
how social media, for example, can help them

00:06:13.280 --> 00:06:14.280
do their work better.

00:06:14.280 --> 00:06:18.580
So we are going to be launching a package
of support for commissioners, focused very

00:06:18.580 --> 00:06:22.610
much on helping them do the very best they
can around participation.

00:06:22.610 --> 00:06:25.030
Health literacy programme
We have launched a health literacy programme,

00:06:25.030 --> 00:06:27.150
which I am hoping I can brief the board on
later.

00:06:27.150 --> 00:06:30.910
It is a commitment to bring 100,000 people
in the most disadvantaged circumstances to

00:06:30.910 --> 00:06:37.290
understand more about how they can use the
internet to take greater control of their

00:06:37.290 --> 00:06:38.290
own health.

00:06:38.290 --> 00:06:42.190
And I should say already that that campaign
has been started, and already nearly 50,000

00:06:42.190 --> 00:06:47.230
people have signed up to going through the
programme of e-leaning and so on.

00:06:47.230 --> 00:06:49.680
It is just an amazing phenomenon, but I am
going to come back and talk about that in

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:50.720
a bit more detail.

00:06:50.720 --> 00:06:55.150
So just some highlights from the participation
guidance, which I hope in our planning guidance

00:06:55.150 --> 00:06:57.930
and in our own business plan, we can really
get behind.

00:06:57.930 --> 00:07:02.790
I think it is a really important legacy and
opportunity for NHS England to promote.

00:07:02.790 --> 00:07:06.250
AGM
Finally, we have the annual general meeting.

00:07:06.250 --> 00:07:08.330
Of course we were all there.

00:07:08.330 --> 00:07:12.010
I think it was a very good example of us really
trying hard to live our values.

00:07:12.010 --> 00:07:15.151
As far as I know it is unprecedented -- I
am unaware of any other board in the public

00:07:15.151 --> 00:07:22.270
service at all that has been so committed
to holding an AGM in such a public context.

00:07:22.270 --> 00:07:26.460
As you know there were 200 people who joined
us in morning sessions to think through some

00:07:26.460 --> 00:07:31.120
of the challenges we should be facing and
tackling. 400 people came to the actual AGM

00:07:31.120 --> 00:07:32.120
itself.

00:07:32.120 --> 00:07:35.640
We had that incredibly moving and I think
robust conversation with some of the work

00:07:35.640 --> 00:07:38.900
stream leads on the platform there.

00:07:38.900 --> 00:07:45.930
Again, just as an indicator, more than 200
Twitter accounts were reached during that

00:07:45.930 --> 00:07:46.930
day.

00:07:46.930 --> 00:07:47.930
That is a lot.

00:07:47.930 --> 00:07:50.340
That is more than the Independent newspaper
sells, or way more than the Guardian sells,

00:07:50.340 --> 00:07:51.340
for example.

00:07:51.340 --> 00:07:55.470
It is properly reaching out to the public
at large.

00:07:55.470 --> 00:08:00.580
And what we have committed to again, in the
paper, is that we will be taking feedback

00:08:00.580 --> 00:08:05.720
from the five themes we looked at during that
day to the expo on March 3rd.

00:08:05.720 --> 00:08:10.330
So this is an ongoing, real commitment to
taking action.

00:08:10.330 --> 00:08:14.280
Not just listening for listening's sake, but
getting on and actually doing what we are

00:08:14.280 --> 00:08:15.280
hearing.

00:08:15.280 --> 00:08:18.050
Those are just some highlights, then, of the
work we are doing in participation.

00:08:18.050 --> 00:08:23.450
I think, however bleak the times might be,
these are things we should be very proud of.

00:08:23.450 --> 00:08:27.580
They are just beginnings, but they are real
beginnings, and what I think I am most excited

00:08:27.580 --> 00:08:32.399
by is the way in which the community is at
large is responding so positively to our request,

00:08:32.399 --> 00:08:33.810
really, for help.

00:08:33.810 --> 00:08:36.080
I think that is something we should be very
positive about.

00:08:36.080 --> 00:08:40.140
Malcolm Grant: Thank you, Tim, and I think
you know that you have the full support of

00:08:40.140 --> 00:08:41.710
all the board on this.

00:08:41.710 --> 00:08:49.640
It only works if all the board buy into it,
and do not see listening to the public voice

00:08:49.640 --> 00:08:52.620
as a listening exercise but actually as a
prompt to action.

00:08:52.620 --> 00:08:57.630
Of those events that you have discussed in
your paper and orally this morning, I must

00:08:57.630 --> 00:09:03.010
say that those I have participated in I have
found exceptionally transformative in my understanding

00:09:03.010 --> 00:09:08.760
of why we sometimes have a mismatch between
what providers think patients want and what

00:09:08.760 --> 00:09:10.400
patients say that they want.

00:09:10.400 --> 00:09:12.930
Especially, I think, in relation to long-term
conditions.

00:09:12.930 --> 00:09:14.300
Thank you for those comments.

00:09:14.300 --> 00:09:16.680
Power shifts and changing public engagement
Citizens' Assembly

00:09:16.680 --> 00:09:23.450
Victor Adebowale: Ciarán and I were very
much involved in the design of the Citizens'

00:09:23.450 --> 00:09:34.870
Assembly, and I just wanted to make the point
that I am very ambitious about this process,

00:09:34.870 --> 00:09:38.780
and that there are two ways these things can
go, in my view.

00:09:38.780 --> 00:09:40.900
One is that we can get people involved.

00:09:40.900 --> 00:09:43.250
I think that is fairly easy, actually.

00:09:43.250 --> 00:09:48.551
Twitter and that stuff is fairly straightforward,
and we have enough people out there who will

00:09:48.551 --> 00:09:49.551
want to engage.

00:09:49.551 --> 00:09:56.301
The really difficult bit is actually engaging
the people who are outside the IT loop, and

00:09:56.301 --> 00:10:01.460
bringing them in, and those outside the normal
discourse, and bringing them in.

00:10:01.460 --> 00:10:03.360
That is difficult.

00:10:03.360 --> 00:10:11.791
The really difficult bit is actually how we
use this Citizens' Assembly, NHS assembly

00:10:11.791 --> 00:10:18.110
stuff, to change behaviours in the NHS, which
is about power.

00:10:18.110 --> 00:10:25.670
So the ambition should be not just to engage
people, but to shift power from where it is

00:10:25.670 --> 00:10:26.680
to where it needs to be.

00:10:26.680 --> 00:10:31.000
What I am interested in, and it is part of
the discussion that we are going to be having

00:10:31.000 --> 00:10:36.400
next Tuesday, is: what are the indicators
that that is actually happening?

00:10:36.400 --> 00:10:39.790
Because I think we could fool ourselves into
thinking that it is real, but it is not.

00:10:39.790 --> 00:10:43.150
Participation
That leads me to my second point, which is

00:10:43.150 --> 00:10:51.070
about the idea of the participation of individuals
in the design of services.

00:10:51.070 --> 00:10:55.500
I think it maps onto the point that David
was making earlier.

00:10:55.500 --> 00:10:59.190
It strikes me that perhaps we are not being
ambitious enough.

00:10:59.190 --> 00:11:06.180
The reason I say that is that out there are
lots of really clear methodologies for engaging

00:11:06.180 --> 00:11:11.290
communities that are reliable, repeatable
and happening, and it would be a shame for

00:11:11.290 --> 00:11:14.800
us to reinvent the wheel -- to make the same
mistakes that other systems have done, which

00:11:14.800 --> 00:11:18.040
is to think that it is not happening, and
then to reinvent something.

00:11:18.040 --> 00:11:23.480
I think we should be learning and promoting
what works and is out there already.

00:11:23.480 --> 00:11:28.730
Secondly, I think we should go beyond simply
the idea of care planning.

00:11:28.730 --> 00:11:35.190
I think that care planning and the idea of
people having a right to a care plan is fantastic,

00:11:35.190 --> 00:11:40.760
but really the big shift is the co-design
of services that people are involved in delivering,

00:11:40.760 --> 00:11:43.230
which, again, is happening out there.

00:11:43.230 --> 00:11:47.100
However, doing that requires quite a significant
power shift.

00:11:47.100 --> 00:11:52.770
So I think we just need to be clear about
the implications of what we have started;

00:11:52.770 --> 00:11:58.740
how we really understand the shift, the needle
of power, because that is what it is about;

00:11:58.740 --> 00:12:04.960
and how we overcome some of the obvious barriers
that are in place to prevent us from doing

00:12:04.960 --> 00:12:05.960
that.

00:12:05.960 --> 00:12:09.630
In other words, we need to have a real discussion
about it and not one that is actually an artefact.

00:12:09.630 --> 00:12:15.380
So I think Tim's leadership on this is fantastic,
but I think we have to push the power issue,

00:12:15.380 --> 00:12:19.390
because that is where tyres hit tarmac.

00:12:19.390 --> 00:12:25.350
Margaret Casely-Hayford: I think my point
is very similar to Victor's, and probably

00:12:25.350 --> 00:12:26.860
will be much less well-put.

00:12:26.860 --> 00:12:34.040
First of all, I really do want to congratulate
you, Tim, and the team, because I actually

00:12:34.040 --> 00:12:37.980
think that the digital revolution is taking
off; it is really exciting.

00:12:37.980 --> 00:12:42.010
However, what is great is that you are also
running that in parallel with the assemblies

00:12:42.010 --> 00:12:48.000
and the panels, so it is very clear that is
not just those who can enter the electronic

00:12:48.000 --> 00:12:52.580
age and speak to us and speak to the NHS,
which is critical.

00:12:52.580 --> 00:12:58.620
There will of course still be people outside
of that, and it goes back to Victor's very

00:12:58.620 --> 00:13:05.280
first point, that we need to make sure that
there is proper investment to make sure they

00:13:05.280 --> 00:13:07.080
are not left behind.

00:13:07.080 --> 00:13:14.000
However, my primary point is really allied
to Victor's point about shifting the power

00:13:14.000 --> 00:13:15.910
base.

00:13:15.910 --> 00:13:21.640
What I think would be really helpful would
be to be able to give back to those who were

00:13:21.640 --> 00:13:28.440
responding to us information about how the
responses are being dealt with -- so that,

00:13:28.440 --> 00:13:33.430
in effect, it really does become a dialogue.

00:13:33.430 --> 00:13:41.600
To use the phrase of one of the attendees
at the AGM, which I think really sums it up

00:13:41.600 --> 00:13:43.560
very well, it is 'we said, you did.'

00:13:43.560 --> 00:13:49.899
So it is very, very clear that we are responding,
the system is responding, because other we

00:13:49.899 --> 00:13:53.490
are talking about nothing in particular.

00:13:53.490 --> 00:13:59.060
Engagement
Ciarán Devane: I think the Future of Health

00:13:59.060 --> 00:14:05.300
conference was a fantastic example of patient
engagement and the clinical engagement and

00:14:05.300 --> 00:14:08.360
the system engagement, at the same time in
the same room.

00:14:08.360 --> 00:14:14.279
I think that where we get that right, we have
a greater number of sustainable solutions.

00:14:14.279 --> 00:14:18.710
I just have this slight worry that we will
do some fantastic stuff over here with the

00:14:18.710 --> 00:14:23.280
Citizens' Assembly; the commissioning assembly
will be in a different room on a different

00:14:23.280 --> 00:14:27.010
day, also doing some brilliant stuff; and
they will not necessarily gel.

00:14:27.010 --> 00:14:31.399
So there is something in this about bringing
all of that together in the right way through

00:14:31.399 --> 00:14:36.690
the call to action process, so we do not end
up with two fantastic engagement process that

00:14:36.690 --> 00:14:44.020
do not quite align or capitalise on the contention
you get when you have different points of

00:14:44.020 --> 00:14:45.020
view.

00:14:45.020 --> 00:14:50.810
Because there are some people out there, like
one of the royal colleges, saying that what

00:14:50.810 --> 00:14:58.149
we need is less engagement and more decision-making
-- presumably by members of that royal college.

00:14:58.149 --> 00:15:03.160
So there are forces of resistance out there,
in that sense, who do not quite see the need

00:15:03.160 --> 00:15:04.670
to do this.

00:15:04.670 --> 00:15:07.350
However, it is fantastic.

00:15:07.350 --> 00:15:12.110
Making change happen
David Nicholson: I think Tim said at one point

00:15:12.110 --> 00:15:15.330
during his presentation that these are tough
times for the NHS.

00:15:15.330 --> 00:15:16.330
And they are.

00:15:16.330 --> 00:15:18.810
People are under pressure, and there are great
challenges, and all the rest of it.

00:15:18.810 --> 00:15:22.700
However, what strikes me about the kinds of
things Tim has been talking about has been

00:15:22.700 --> 00:15:25.320
reflected to me over the last week or so,
because I have been to two things.

00:15:25.320 --> 00:15:30.480
I have been to a values summit -- NHS England
have been sponsoring a series of values summits

00:15:30.480 --> 00:15:31.480
around the country.

00:15:31.480 --> 00:15:33.810
We had 400 people at the one in London.

00:15:33.810 --> 00:15:35.030
I also did the one in Manchester.

00:15:35.030 --> 00:15:42.010
There was this huge energy; people want to
engage in this and change.

00:15:42.010 --> 00:15:46.690
The NHS is not all about inspections and regulation
and all of that.

00:15:46.690 --> 00:15:51.950
There is a huge amount of energy amongst the
people who both use the NHS and the people

00:15:51.950 --> 00:15:56.480
who work in it to make a difference in the
future.

00:15:56.480 --> 00:16:02.250
We had the launch of NHS Change Day earlier
this week as well, which was a massive, bottom

00:16:02.250 --> 00:16:08.780
up attempt by, first of all, a small group
of clinicians, but now it has expanded across

00:16:08.780 --> 00:16:09.780
England.

00:16:09.780 --> 00:16:17.440
Nearly 190,000 people pledged a change last
time, and the plan this year is to generate

00:16:17.440 --> 00:16:19.130
half a million of these.

00:16:19.130 --> 00:16:23.270
There is this huge energy from front-line
staff and people to make things happen and

00:16:23.270 --> 00:16:24.270
make change happen.

00:16:24.270 --> 00:16:28.120
The sorts of things Tim is talking about help
and encourage this in a really fantastic way,

00:16:28.120 --> 00:16:32.680
so I think we should be really positive and
get behind all of this, because this is the

00:16:32.680 --> 00:16:33.779
future of the NHS.

00:16:33.779 --> 00:16:38.899
We are not going to change the quality and
the outcomes for patients and the power relations

00:16:38.899 --> 00:16:40.700
by systems and processes.

00:16:40.700 --> 00:16:45.970
We are going to change it by inspiring and
motivating people to get engaged and take

00:16:45.970 --> 00:16:46.970
that power.

00:16:46.970 --> 00:16:48.060
I want to reinforce that.

00:16:48.060 --> 00:16:50.240
This is a really positive thing.

00:16:50.240 --> 00:16:57.410
On the other side, Tim, when you originally
pitched the idea of a Citizens' Assembly,

00:16:57.410 --> 00:17:02.580
you said it could be led by people like Cheryl
Cole, and I just wondered whether you had

00:17:02.580 --> 00:17:07.919
invited her to join and whether she had responded
to that invitation.

00:17:07.919 --> 00:17:14.339
Tim Kelsey: Well, the answer is that we have
not yet engaged Cheryl!

00:17:14.339 --> 00:17:20.850
However, we are heading towards a really meaningful
thought about how we can crystallise some

00:17:20.850 --> 00:17:25.069
of the voices that the community has, some
of the views they have, and we will have to

00:17:25.069 --> 00:17:27.350
see whether she is interested in due course.

00:17:27.350 --> 00:17:29.610
Engaging the public
I would like to very quickly respond to a

00:17:29.610 --> 00:17:32.619
couple of points that have been made.

00:17:32.619 --> 00:17:36.750
I have been advocating ever since I started
(and before) that unleashing the power of

00:17:36.750 --> 00:17:40.390
people is the long-term solution to the sustainability
of the health service, however you describe

00:17:40.390 --> 00:17:41.390
that.

00:17:41.390 --> 00:17:47.000
I believe we are absolutely at the tipping-point
of a movement, with people feeling sufficiently

00:17:47.000 --> 00:17:50.549
empowered to start speaking up, to hold the
local commissioner to account.

00:17:50.549 --> 00:17:54.649
We have Health Watch and all sorts of other
networks that just amplify that opportunity.

00:17:54.649 --> 00:17:57.610
So one thing I wanted to say is that in no
way is anything we are doing trying to reproduce

00:17:57.610 --> 00:17:58.690
what people have already done.

00:17:58.690 --> 00:18:05.950
There is a fantastically rich community of
voices of advocacy that exists in the NHS,

00:18:05.950 --> 00:18:09.090
and in health and care properly.

00:18:09.090 --> 00:18:13.989
To be honest, we have not made enough use
of those many networks.

00:18:13.989 --> 00:18:17.820
So one of the really important things about
this is not duplicating or monopolising in

00:18:17.820 --> 00:18:18.830
any way at all.

00:18:18.830 --> 00:18:25.710
It is absolutely about building on the networks
that exist out there already to inform us

00:18:25.710 --> 00:18:26.710
as commissioners.

00:18:26.710 --> 00:18:30.639
However, I think it is really important, and
it is a big challenge for us, that we recognise

00:18:30.639 --> 00:18:37.149
that in order to persuade our own folk of
the importance of this, as they struggle with

00:18:37.149 --> 00:18:44.200
all those very really challenges just to get
things done, that we crystallise these commitments

00:18:44.200 --> 00:18:45.869
in ways that are measurable.

00:18:45.869 --> 00:18:50.779
I think it is very, very important that, in
the context of the process around the planning

00:18:50.779 --> 00:18:54.369
guidance and our own business plan, we come
up with some reasonably hard-edged measures

00:18:54.369 --> 00:18:58.850
that try to capture how we can hold ourselves
to account for doing this properly.

00:18:58.850 --> 00:19:01.799
That is an important commitment we should
make.

00:19:01.799 --> 00:19:05.120
Malcolm Grant: Thank you very much, Tim, and
to echo the views of other members of the

00:19:05.120 --> 00:19:13.590
board, the classical model of public participation
always was that it was a time of furious activity

00:19:13.590 --> 00:19:15.559
and public voices were listened to.

00:19:15.559 --> 00:19:17.120
Then it stopped.

00:19:17.120 --> 00:19:20.299
The voices were listened to, taken into a
plan, and then the plan led everything.

00:19:20.299 --> 00:19:23.049
What is so different about this is the time
element.

00:19:23.049 --> 00:19:28.140
This is the beginning of a continuing process,
not a one-off.

00:19:28.140 --> 00:19:34.119
I think one is very taken, now, by listening
to not only the voice of patients but also

00:19:34.119 --> 00:19:37.419
the responses we are starting to hear from
the commissioning community about how they

00:19:37.419 --> 00:19:44.010
adapt changing patterns of care to changing
preferences and priorities of patients.

00:19:44.010 --> 00:19:45.480
So thank you for your work on that.

00:19:45.480 --> 00:19:48.460
You have, I think, a presentation for us now.

00:19:48.460 --> 00:19:56.289
Tim Kelsey: Again, right at the beginning
we wanted to offer people the opportunity

00:19:56.289 --> 00:20:03.279
to be part of the commissioning family -- to
actually offer feedback and comment on the

00:20:03.279 --> 00:20:08.200
quality of local services, so that those service
providers could fix the problem and could

00:20:08.200 --> 00:20:10.200
address those issues as quickly as possible.

00:20:10.200 --> 00:20:15.989
In a way, it seems strange this is not already
out there, given the existence of the internet,

00:20:15.989 --> 00:20:18.580
which is a very low-cost technology.

00:20:18.580 --> 00:20:23.279
Parts of our community are already very involved,
as we have just learnt, with Twitter and social

00:20:23.279 --> 00:20:24.279
media.

00:20:24.279 --> 00:20:31.880
So we should really make use as far as we
can of people's willingness to engage in the

00:20:31.880 --> 00:20:34.360
online world.

00:20:34.360 --> 00:20:35.640
Care Connect
Care Connect, I think, could turn out to be

00:20:35.640 --> 00:20:39.929
one of the very important innovations we have
initiated.

00:20:39.929 --> 00:20:44.210
The idea is to provide a multi-channel point
of access -- you can ring it, you can tweet,

00:20:44.210 --> 00:20:49.240
you can email it -- to give people the biggest
possible opportunity to just comment about

00:20:49.240 --> 00:20:53.220
their local services, to find out more about
them -- certainly not just to criticise.

00:20:53.220 --> 00:20:57.360
In fact, most of the comments we get are not
critical comments; they are very positive

00:20:57.360 --> 00:20:58.360
comments.

00:20:58.360 --> 00:21:02.279
However, it is also a means by which a local
hospital or a local GP practice in due course

00:21:02.279 --> 00:21:07.710
can properly engage with their local communities
and have a real-time conversation of the sort

00:21:07.710 --> 00:21:11.730
that we are used to in so many other parts
of our lives.

00:21:11.730 --> 00:21:14.769
We talked about this briefly at the last board
meeting, and I was just going to quickly show

00:21:14.769 --> 00:21:16.649
you what is currently a pilot.

00:21:16.649 --> 00:21:22.799
This is currently available only to people
who are engaging with 17 hospitals in London

00:21:22.799 --> 00:21:25.019
and three in the North East.

00:21:25.019 --> 00:21:30.359
We have been working with a variety of colleagues
across the health and care system -- particularly

00:21:30.359 --> 00:21:34.041
regulators of complaints like the ombudsman
and the GMC -- to investigate whether or not

00:21:34.041 --> 00:21:38.859
this could be a sort of front door for them
as well; whether we can help streamline the

00:21:38.859 --> 00:21:42.590
process by which people who want to make an
actual complaint, as well as a comment, can

00:21:42.590 --> 00:21:46.549
do so much more easily than is currently the
case.

00:21:46.549 --> 00:21:49.830
So I am just going to quickly show you how
this works.

00:21:49.830 --> 00:21:56.440
This is actually accessible through NHS Choices,
the health and information service the already

00:21:56.440 --> 00:21:57.440
exists.

00:21:57.440 --> 00:22:03.860
You can see that there are three core offers
-- just share your experience with other patients,

00:22:03.860 --> 00:22:08.789
rather like Trip Advisor; just free text,
comment, actually offering people the chance

00:22:08.789 --> 00:22:10.799
to find out more about local services.

00:22:10.799 --> 00:22:15.399
One day Barbara and I hope that this will
become an integral part of 111, which can

00:22:15.399 --> 00:22:18.289
offer these sorts of opportunities as well.

00:22:18.289 --> 00:22:23.230
Then there is 'Report a Problem', which is
the bit I am going to show you today, which

00:22:23.230 --> 00:22:28.289
is really encouraging people to be upfront
with us about services when they are not going

00:22:28.289 --> 00:22:29.289
brilliantly well.

00:22:29.289 --> 00:22:34.749
What we know from Francis and from Don Berwick's
report is that capturing the small things

00:22:34.749 --> 00:22:41.820
-- the indignities of failures in basic customer
service -- is how you can turn things around

00:22:41.820 --> 00:22:43.389
before they start going really wrong.

00:22:43.389 --> 00:22:47.130
So this really is about encouraging people
to just tell us the small things -- the room

00:22:47.130 --> 00:23:00.890
was hot or cold, and so on; things on that
sort of level.

00:23:00.890 --> 00:23:04.360
So this is a problem number, which I am just
going to put in here.

00:23:04.360 --> 00:23:07.029
So you have made your problem -- and I will
show you how you can do that -- and you have

00:23:07.029 --> 00:23:08.240
given your comment.

00:23:08.240 --> 00:23:10.080
This is one that exists; it is a real one.

00:23:10.080 --> 00:23:11.820
I am just going to go straight to it.

00:23:11.820 --> 00:23:17.710
So here in St. Helier, somebody has complained,
I think about quite a serious issue in relation

00:23:17.710 --> 00:23:25.549
to the treatment of their mother.

00:23:25.549 --> 00:23:29.410
So this is really about the way in which the
mother was treated, and it was clinically

00:23:29.410 --> 00:23:33.220
inappropriate, even subsequently according
to the lead clinician in the ward.

00:23:33.220 --> 00:23:38.629
This is the daughter, I think, writing in
and expressing her anxiety about the quality

00:23:38.629 --> 00:23:41.999
of care her mother was receiving.

00:23:41.999 --> 00:23:47.559
The good news is that very, very quickly the
hospital gets on the case.

00:23:47.559 --> 00:23:51.429
The idea of this, of course, is that it is
real time, and the hospital can then see the

00:23:51.429 --> 00:23:52.429
comment.

00:23:52.429 --> 00:23:57.009
As you can see beneath that, they acted quickly
to engage with the lady making the comment

00:23:57.009 --> 00:24:02.139
and then met with them and obviously got to
a point of resolution.

00:24:02.139 --> 00:24:08.609
That has now been closed as a complaint but
is still visible and will remain visible forever

00:24:08.609 --> 00:24:13.649
on the service.

00:24:13.649 --> 00:24:19.120
Those complaints are also presented on a map,
and this is really the key part of the power

00:24:19.120 --> 00:24:25.679
of this, because most people would look at
this by going into their local provider and

00:24:25.679 --> 00:24:28.760
observing how many problems are on the map
at any one time.

00:24:28.760 --> 00:24:33.980
The colours change, so if you were to scroll
out and widen the geography, you could see

00:24:33.980 --> 00:24:42.340
in your local area hotspots where you had
large numbers of active complaints.

00:24:42.340 --> 00:24:46.669
We believe this will become a very important
tool for local commissioners for managing

00:24:46.669 --> 00:24:49.679
in real time the quality of their local services.

00:24:49.679 --> 00:24:53.090
Obviously at the moment we have a relatively
small number of active comments because we

00:24:53.090 --> 00:24:58.429
have not promoted it, but you can just begin
to see how the future would look.

00:24:58.429 --> 00:25:03.970
This is a technique that is used in most American
cities to improve the quality of their local

00:25:03.970 --> 00:25:04.970
services.

00:25:04.970 --> 00:25:15.129
So if you can just imagine on this map of
London you had the hotspots and so on.

00:25:15.129 --> 00:25:21.419
The other important thing here is that we
have also now built into this a link to the

00:25:21.419 --> 00:25:27.929
Friends and Family Test, so in due course
providers might choose to encourage people

00:25:27.929 --> 00:25:33.409
to comment over Care Connect, and also link
directly to the Friends and Family Test score

00:25:33.409 --> 00:25:38.409
that their hospital is being given by local
customers or local patients.

00:25:38.409 --> 00:25:40.690
So this is, I think, a glimpse of the future.

00:25:40.690 --> 00:25:46.630
It is very early days, but I just wanted to
give the board a chance to see it.

00:25:46.630 --> 00:25:52.159
Malcolm Grant: How do people access this?

00:25:52.159 --> 00:25:58.799
Tim Kelsey: If you are in one of the pilot
areas, you are actively, individually offered

00:25:58.799 --> 00:26:00.669
the opportunity of engaging.

00:26:00.669 --> 00:26:01.830
You actually just access it online.

00:26:01.830 --> 00:26:03.860
It is free; anyone can see it.

00:26:03.860 --> 00:26:08.429
It is just off the NHS Choices website as
things stand.

00:26:08.429 --> 00:26:13.919
I might just add, actually, that the popularity
of this kind of opportunity is demonstrated

00:26:13.919 --> 00:26:20.749
by another fact, which is that NHS Choices
recently recorded a new volume of activity.

00:26:20.749 --> 00:26:27.399
Today, more than 1 million people each day
are accessing NHS Choices for all sorts of

00:26:27.399 --> 00:26:32.649
different purposes -- by far the largest health
information resources of its type anywhere

00:26:32.649 --> 00:26:34.470
in the world.

00:26:34.470 --> 00:26:40.129
So I am sure that when we launch this nationally,
as we propose to do during the course of next

00:26:40.129 --> 00:26:45.970
year, we will find people embracing it and
hopefully engaging quite fully with it.

00:26:45.970 --> 00:26:50.509
David Nicholson: I have been talking to some
chief executives of hospitals who are now

00:26:50.509 --> 00:26:55.700
online with all of this, and it does fundamentally
challenge and change the way they do their

00:26:55.700 --> 00:26:56.700
job.

00:26:56.700 --> 00:26:57.739
They say that.

00:26:57.739 --> 00:27:03.109
For the first time, they can have on their
screen in real time a whole set of responses

00:27:03.109 --> 00:27:09.080
from patients about issues; they can operationally
engage and involve in a way they simply could

00:27:09.080 --> 00:27:10.369
not in the past.

00:27:10.369 --> 00:27:13.930
It challenges the way they run their hospitals
in what I think is a really productive way.

00:27:13.930 --> 00:27:18.240
So I think this is a really, really big change.

00:27:18.240 --> 00:27:25.429
Malcolm Grant: It empowers leadership and
management in institutions.

00:27:25.429 --> 00:27:32.570
Thank you very much, Tim, and please pass
on our thanks to your team for the innovative

00:27:32.570 --> 00:27:34.250
nature of the work.

